---
import { cacheFor } from '../lib/cache.ts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

cacheFor(Astro.response, 600);
---
<Base title="About · Equilux">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About Equilux</h1>
    <p>
      Equilux shows when the sun rises and sets anywhere in the world, and how that changes over the
      year. Search for a place, or click anywhere on the map.
    </p>
    <p>
      The equilux is the day when daylight and darkness are exactly equal. It isn’t the equinox: the
      atmosphere bends sunlight over the horizon, and sunrise counts from the top of the sun rather
      than its middle, so on the equinox itself the day is a few minutes longer than the night. The
      equilux comes a few days before the March equinox and a few days after the September one in
      the Northern Hemisphere; this is reversed in the Southern Hemisphere. Near the equator, daylight
      can stay longer than 12 hours all year, so there is no equilux.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the chart</h2>
    <p>
      Each column is a day, from midnight at the bottom to midnight at the top, in local clock time.
      The lightest band is daylight; the bands around it are the three stages of twilight, darker as
      the sun sinks further below the horizon; the darkest is night. Where the clocks change, the bands
      jump an hour. Vertical dashed lines mark solstices, equinoxes, and the local equiluxes.
      An equilux marker picks the calendar day closest to 12 hours of daylight where day length crosses
      that threshold. Hover over a marker’s date to see its details.
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Sunrise and sunset</dt>
      <dd>When the top of the sun is on the horizon, allowing for the atmosphere bending its light.</dd>
      <dt>Civil twilight</dt>
      <dd>The sun up to 6° below the horizon: light enough to be outdoors without lights. Its start and end are first and last light.</dd>
      <dt>Nautical twilight</dt>
      <dd>6° to 12° below: the horizon at sea is still visible, and the brighter stars are out.</dd>
      <dt>Astronomical twilight</dt>
      <dd>12° to 18° below: dark to the eye, but not yet dark enough for astronomers.</dd>
      <dt>Golden hour</dt>
      <dd>Here, the sun less than 6° up before sunset: the warm, low light photographers like.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Times are calculated, not looked up, and should be good to a minute or two. Hills, buildings and
      unusual weather can make the real thing differ a little.
    </p>

    <h2>Places and time zones</h2>
    <p>
      Places come from <a href="https://www.geonames.org/">GeoNames</a>: every town and village with at least
      500 people. A point picked on the map takes its time zone from the nearest of them; far out to sea,
      it uses nautical time, an hour for every 15° of longitude.
    </p>

    <h2>History</h2>
    <p>
      Equilux began in 2007 as Sunlightr, written in Perl on Catalyst, with a three-step form and a
      table of times. It was rebuilt and renamed in 2026, with Astro on Cloudflare, adding the map and
      the charts. It still matches place names the same way.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
